#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# cycles

#% text
# `cycles([[p1, p2, ...], ...])`: the permutation with these disjoint cycles, in canonical form

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A permutation is a list of disjoint cycles of positive integers; `cycles` rotates each cycle to start at its least point, orders the cycles by that point and drops fixed points, so equal permutations are equal expressions.

#% item
# Cycles that share a point leave the call unevaluated.

#% item
# `cycles([])` is the identity.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
cycles([[3, 1, 2], [5, 4], [6]])
#% output
#= cycles([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5]])

#% cell
cycles([])
#% output
#= cycles([])

#% cell
permutation_list(cycles([[1, 4], [2, 3]]))
#% output
#= [4, 3, 2, 1]

